<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Russia from Rus' to Revolution</h1><p class="path-tagline">The Russian state from the princes of Kiev to the Soviet Union and its end.</p><p class="path-intro">The first great East Slavic state was Kievan Rus', ruled by the Rurikid princes from Kiev on the river route from the Baltic to Byzantium. These stops follow its conversion to Orthodox Christianity, the merchant republic of Novgorod, the Mongol conquest and the rise of Moscow, which gathered the Russian lands and threw off Tatar rule. They continue through the tsars from Ivan IV to Peter the Great, the Russian Empire that grew to some 22.8 million square kilometres, and the revolution of 1917. The last stop is the Soviet Union, founded in 1922 and dissolved in 1991.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Strategist</a> · <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kievan-rus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-gate-kyiv.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 882 CE – 1240 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kievan Rus'</span><span class="path-step-why">Rurikid princes ruled the first great East Slavic state from Kiev, a federation of principalities on the river route from the Baltic to Byzantium, until the Mongols sacked Kiev in 1240.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Kievan Rus' was a medieval East Slavic state centred on Kyiv, flourishing from the late ninth to the thirteenth century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 958–1015</span><span class="path-step-name">Vladimir the Great</span><span class="path-step-why">In 988 Vladimir the Great accepted Christianity from Byzantium and married the emperor's sister Anna, bringing churches, Church Slavonic writing and Byzantine art to Kievan Rus'.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Vladimir the Great was Grand Prince of Kiev from about 980 to 1015.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/novgorod-republic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-of-the-transfiguration-on-ilyina-street.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 1136 CE – 1478 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Novgorod Republic</span><span class="path-step-why">This merchant city-state of the Russian north elected its officials and archbishop, hired princes by contract and traded furs with the Hanseatic League until Ivan III annexed it in 1478.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-kievan-rus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-kievan-rus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1223–1242</span><span class="path-step-name">Mongol invasion of Kievan Rus'</span><span class="path-step-why">Batu Khan's conquest of 1237–1240 destroyed Ryazan, Vladimir and many other cities, and the Rus' principalities then paid tribute to the Golden Horde for some two centuries.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Mongol invasion of Kievan Rus' began with a raid that defeated the Rus' princes at the Kalka River in 1223 and became a full conquest under Batu Khan in 1237–1240.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dormition-cathedral-moscow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 1263 CE – 1547 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Grand Duchy of Moscow</span><span class="path-step-why">A small appanage on the Moskva River grew rich collecting tribute for the Golden Horde, ended Tatar overlordship at the Ugra River in 1480 and annexed Novgorod and Tver.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Grand Duchy of Moscow, or Muscovy, was the Russian principality that rose from the fourteenth century to dominate the Rus' lands and throw off Mongol overlordship.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-square.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 1547 CE – 1721 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Tsardom of Russia</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded when Ivan IV was crowned the first Tsar of All Russia in 1547, the Tsardom conquered the Volga khanates and crossed Siberia to the Pacific.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1672–1725</span><span class="path-step-name">Peter the Great</span><span class="path-step-why">Proclaimed emperor in 1721 after victory over Sweden, Peter reorganised the army, built a navy, founded St Petersburg and subordinated the Church to the state.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Peter the Great was Tsar and first Emperor of Russia, proclaimed emperor in 1721 after victory over Sweden in the Great Northern War.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/russian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1721 CE – 1917 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Russian Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Grown to some 22.8 million square kilometres from the Baltic to the Pacific, the empire freed some 23 million serfs in 1861 by Alexander II's Emancipation Edict.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Russian Empire was the state ruled by the Romanov dynasty from Peter the Great's proclamation of the empire in 1721 until the revolution of 1917.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russian-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1917</span><span class="path-step-name">Russian Revolution</span><span class="path-step-why">The February Revolution of 1917 forced Nicholas II to abdicate, and in the October Revolution the Bolsheviks under Lenin seized power, then won the civil war that followed.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Russian Revolution of 1917 brought down the Russian monarchy and led to the first communist state.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/soviet-union"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1922 CE – 1991 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Soviet Union</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded in 1922 as the first communist state, it industrialised under Stalin at enormous human cost, rivalled the United States as a superpower and was dissolved in 1991.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Soviet Union was a communist state that existed from 1922 to 1991, established on the territory of the former Russian Empire after the revolutions of 1917.</span></span></a></li></ol>
